{
  "dom_id": "step-q87636",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178864/assets/81c7a4f9f0b9ab855b22ad7895f5f0d7b1ae3711563e9691117dfd99f878dccb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$",
  "worked_solution": "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a pentagon (it has $5$ sides). So, the polygons $S,$ $T,$ and $U$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $5$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $Q$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $5$ sides) because $Q$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has two right angles, while $Q$ doesn't have right angles.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $R$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅R.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
